None at this time - I am just preparing to work on an application that is expected to work with 3rd party api's and applications and wanted to verify if java handled conflicts natively somehow but this verifies that it does not.  i.e. I think the only safe way to use 3rd party apps in the same jvm is to implement a classloader strategy for isolation.

You're absolutely correct! Java does not natively handle version conflicts - it uses a "first class wins" model where whichever version of a class is loaded first is the one used throughout the JVM.

Your assessment is spot on:

For true isolation when integrating 3rd party applications with potentially conflicting dependencies, custom classloaders are indeed the proper solution.

Classloader isolation strategies:

1. Parent-Last (Child-First) ClassLoader:

Load classes from the child classloader before delegating to parent:

java
public class IsolatedClassLoader extends URLClassLoader {
    public IsolatedClassLoader(URL[] urls, ClassLoader parent) {
        super(urls, parent);
    }
    
    @Override
    public Class<?> loadClass(String name) throws ClassNotFoundException {
        // Check if already loaded
        Class<?> c = findLoadedClass(name);
        if (c != null) {
            return c;
        }
        
        // Try to load from this classloader first (child-first)
        // Skip java.* and javax.* which must come from parent
        if (!name.startsWith("java.") && !name.startsWith("javax.")) {
            try {
                return findClass(name);
            } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
                // Fall through to parent
            }
        }
        
        // Delegate to parent
        return super.loadClass(name);
    }
}
2. Isolated Plugin Architecture:

java
public class PluginManager {
    private Map<String, ClassLoader> pluginLoaders = new HashMap<>();
    
    public void loadPlugin(String pluginName, File jarFile) throws Exception {
        URL[] urls = { jarFile.toURI().toURL() };
        
        // Create isolated classloader for this plugin
        ClassLoader pluginLoader = new URLClassLoader(
            urls,
            getClass().getClassLoader().getParent() // Skip application classloader
        );
        
        pluginLoaders.put(pluginName, pluginLoader);
    }
    
    public Object createPluginInstance(String pluginName, String className) 
            throws Exception {
        ClassLoader loader = pluginLoaders.get(pluginName);
        Class<?> clazz = loader.loadClass(className);
        return clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
    }
}
3. OSGi Framework (industry standard):

OSGi provides sophisticated classloader isolation out of the box:

xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.felix</groupId>
    <artifactId>org.apache.felix.framework</artifactId>
    <version>7.0.5</version>
</dependency>
Each OSGi bundle runs in its own classloader with explicit import/export declarations.

4. Java 9+ Module System (JPMS):

Java Platform Module System provides some isolation:

java
// module-info.java
module my.plugin {
    requires transitive some.api;
    exports my.plugin.api;
}
But it's not as flexible as custom classloaders for your use case.

Real-world example - Plugin with isolated dependencies:

java
public class Application {
    public void runIsolatedPlugin(File pluginJar) throws Exception {
        // Create isolated classloader
        URL[] urls = { pluginJar.toURI().toURL() };
        URLClassLoader pluginLoader = new URLClassLoader(urls, null); // null parent = bootstrap only
        
        // Load and run plugin class
        Class<?> pluginClass = pluginLoader.loadClass("com.thirdparty.Plugin");
        Object plugin = pluginClass.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
        
        // Invoke plugin method via reflection
        Method runMethod = pluginClass.getMethod("run");
        runMethod.invoke(plugin);
        
        // Clean up
        pluginLoader.close();
    }
}
Challenges with classloader isolation:

Shared interfaces - If plugins need to communicate, you need a shared API in the parent classloader
Serialization - Objects from different classloaders aren't directly compatible
Static state - Static variables are per-classloader, not per-JVM
Native libraries - Can only be loaded once per JVM
Thread context classloaders - Need careful management
When to use classloader isolation:

✅ Yes - use classloaders when:

Integrating multiple 3rd party applications with conflicting dependencies
Building plugin architectures
Running untrusted code
Need true version isolation
❌ No - dependency management sufficient when:

You control all the code
Dependencies are compatible
Simple library conflicts (use exclusions/dependencyManagement)
Your conclusion is correct: For your scenario (integrating 3rd party apps), classloader isolation is the professional, robust solution. Maven dependency management only works when you have compatible versions or can control the dependencies.

Recommended approach for your use case:

Either:

Custom classloader strategy (full control, more work)
OSGi framework (battle-tested, steeper learning curve)
Separate JVM processes (ultimate isolation, IPC overhead)
Which direction are you leaning for your architecture?

In the long run, all three actually.  In the short term, probably a combination of #1 and #3.  I am developing a pseudo-desktop that can both launch external processes (java or native) as well as hopefully host some java apps within the same jvm.  I am currently using java 8 so that might rule out osgi? (although I will be forced in the coming year or two to make this java9+ compliant)  my initial research led me to the 3rd party api called PF4J (https://github.com/pf4j/pf4j) which does seem promising but it requires your plugins to be built with/around that framework - that requirement is a bit more than I was initially wanting since my "perfect world" goal is that I can simply download 3rd party .jars/.zips and use them as-is.

Great architecture vision! Your assessment is solid. Let me address your points:

OSGi and Java 8: OSGi absolutely works with Java 8 - Apache Felix, Eclipse Equinox, etc. all support Java 8. However, OSGi has the same issue as PF4J: it requires bundles to be OSGi-aware with manifest metadata, so it doesn't meet your "use as-is" requirement.

The "use arbitrary JARs as-is" challenge:

This is the fundamental tension - true plugin frameworks require cooperation from the plugin, but you want to treat any JAR as a plugin without modification.

Strategies for "as-is" JAR usage:

1. Convention-based discovery with isolated classloaders:

java
public class AsIsPluginLoader {
    
    public LoadedPlugin loadJar(File jarFile) throws Exception {
        // Create isolated classloader
        URL[] urls = { jarFile.toURI().toURL() };
        URLClassLoader loader = new URLClassLoader(urls, 
            ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader().getParent());
        
        // Convention: look for Main-Class in manifest
        JarFile jar = new JarFile(jarFile);
        Manifest manifest = jar.getManifest();
        String mainClass = manifest.getMainAttributes()
            .getValue("Main-Class");
        
        if (mainClass != null) {
            Class<?> clazz = loader.loadClass(mainClass);
            return new LoadedPlugin(loader, clazz);
        }
        
        // Alternative: scan for classes implementing known interface
        // or just expose the classloader for manual usage
        return new LoadedPlugin(loader, null);
    }
    
    public static class LoadedPlugin {
        private final URLClassLoader classLoader;
        private final Class<?> entryPoint;
        
        public LoadedPlugin(URLClassLoader classLoader, Class<?> entryPoint) {
            this.classLoader = classLoader;
            this.entryPoint = entryPoint;
        }
        
        public void run(String[] args) throws Exception {
            if (entryPoint != null) {
                Method main = entryPoint.getMethod("main", String[].class);
                main.invoke(null, (Object) args);
            }
        }
        
        public URLClassLoader getClassLoader() {
            return classLoader;
        }
    }
}
2. Wrapper approach - JAR stays untouched:

java
// User creates a tiny descriptor file alongside the JAR
// plugins/thirdparty-app/plugin.properties:
jar=thirdparty-app.jar
mainClass=com.vendor.Application
classpath=lib/dependency1.jar,lib/dependency2.jar

// Your loader reads this and sets up classloader
public class DescriptorBasedLoader {
    public LoadedPlugin load(File pluginDir) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.load(new FileInputStream(
            new File(pluginDir, "plugin.properties")));
        
        // Build classpath from descriptor
        List<URL> urls = new ArrayList<>();
        urls.add(new File(pluginDir, props.getProperty("jar"))
            .toURI().toURL());
        
        String classpath = props.getProperty("classpath", "");
        for (String cp : classpath.split(",")) {
            if (!cp.trim().isEmpty()) {
                urls.add(new File(pluginDir, cp.trim())
                    .toURI().toURL());
            }
        }
        
        URLClassLoader loader = new URLClassLoader(
            urls.toArray(new URL[0]),
            ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader().getParent());
        
        String mainClass = props.getProperty("mainClass");
        return new LoadedPlugin(loader, 
            loader.loadClass(mainClass));
    }
}
3. Hybrid: Process by default, in-JVM when safe:

java
public class PluginLauncher {
    
    public void launch(File jarFile, boolean isolated) throws Exception {
        if (isolated) {
            // Launch in separate JVM process
            ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(
                "java", "-jar", jarFile.getAbsolutePath());
            Process process = pb.start();
            // Handle process I/O, lifecycle
        } else {
            // Load in same JVM with isolated classloader
            AsIsPluginLoader loader = new AsIsPluginLoader();
            LoadedPlugin plugin = loader.loadJar(jarFile);
            plugin.run(new String[0]);
        }
    }
}
```

**4. Your pseudo-desktop architecture suggestion:**
```
MyDesktop/
├── core/                          # Your desktop framework
│   ├── PluginRegistry
│   ├── ProcessManager            # For external processes
│   └── IsolatedClassLoaderManager # For in-JVM
│
├── plugins/                       # Plugin directory
│   ├── app1/
