Practical hybrid strategy:

java
public class DesktopPluginManager {
    
    public enum LaunchMode {
        EXTERNAL_PROCESS,    // Always separate JVM
        IN_JVM_ISOLATED,     // Same JVM, isolated classloader
        IN_JVM_SHARED,       # Same JVM, shared classloader (risky)
        AUTO                 // Detect and choose
    }
    
    public Application launch(File pluginPath, LaunchMode mode) 
            throws Exception {
        
        // Auto-detect
        if (mode == LaunchMode.AUTO) {
            mode = detectBestMode(pluginPath);
        }
        
        switch (mode) {
            case EXTERNAL_PROCESS:
                return launchExternalProcess(pluginPath);
            
            case IN_JVM_ISOLATED:
                return launchIsolated(pluginPath);
            
            case IN_JVM_SHARED:
                return launchShared(pluginPath);
            
            default:
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown mode");
        }
    }
    
    private LaunchMode detectBestMode(File pluginPath) {
        // Check for known conflict indicators
        // Check size/complexity
        // Check if native code present
        // Default to EXTERNAL_PROCESS for safety
        return LaunchMode.EXTERNAL_PROCESS;
    }
    
    private Application launchExternalProcess(File jarFile) 
            throws Exception {
        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(
            System.getProperty("java.home") + "/bin/java",
            "-jar", jarFile.getAbsolutePath()
        );
        Process process = pb.start();
        return new ExternalApplication(process);
    }
    
    private Application launchIsolated(File jarFile) 
            throws Exception {
        URL[] urls = { jarFile.toURI().toURL() };
        URLClassLoader loader = new URLClassLoader(urls, null);
        
        // Convention-based discovery
        JarFile jar = new JarFile(jarFile);
        String mainClass = jar.getManifest()
            .getMainAttributes().getValue("Main-Class");
        
        Class<?> clazz = loader.loadClass(mainClass);
        return new InJvmApplication(loader, clazz);
    }
}
Recommendation for your use case:

Since you want "as-is" JARs without modification:

Start simple: External process by default (safest, zero conflicts)
Add isolated classloader support for JARs you trust/test
Use conventions rather than frameworks:
Discover Main-Class from manifest
Optional .desktop or .plugin metadata file for hints
User can specify launch mode via UI
Manual classification: Let users mark apps as "safe for in-JVM" after testing
Skip PF4J/OSGi for now - they're great for controlled plugin ecosystems, but don't meet your "arbitrary JAR" requirement. You can always add framework support later for plugins that opt-in.

Your short-term path:

External process launcher (easy, works for everything)
Isolated classloader for cooperative JARs (more work, better integration)
Simple conventions (Main-Class, optional metadata)
This gives you the flexibility to evolve toward OSGi/PF4J later if you build a plugin ecosystem, while supporting arbitrary JARs today.

