Does this match what you had in mind?

Yes, very much.  However, to rewind a bit before we proceed - I suppose a drawback to isolated plugins is the extra memory consumption for independent classloaders?

Yes, absolutely - that's a real consideration, though the impact varies significantly depending on what you're loading.

Memory overhead breakdown:

1. ClassLoader object itself:

Minimal (few KB per classloader)
Not a concern even with dozens of plugins
2. Loaded classes (the real cost):

Each class definition: ~1-2 KB minimum + method bytecode
Class metadata (Method/Field objects, etc.)
This is where duplication hurts
3. What gets duplicated across classloaders:

If Plugin A and Plugin B both use Apache Commons Lang:

Shared classloader approach:
├── commons-lang3.jar (500 KB, ~100 classes loaded)
└── Total: 500 KB

Isolated classloader approach:
├── Plugin A ClassLoader
│   └── commons-lang3.jar (500 KB, ~100 classes)
├── Plugin B ClassLoader
│   └── commons-lang3.jar (500 KB, ~100 classes)
└── Total: 1 MB (duplicated!)
Real-world impact examples:

Small plugin (simple utility):

JAR: 50 KB
Typical classes loaded: 20-30
Memory overhead: ~100-200 KB per instance
Impact: Negligible
Medium plugin (Swing app with some libraries):

JAR + dependencies: 2-5 MB
Typical classes loaded: 500-1000
Memory overhead: ~5-10 MB per instance
Impact: Noticeable with many plugins
Large plugin (complex app with many dependencies):

JAR + dependencies: 20-50 MB
Typical classes loaded: 3000-5000+
Memory overhead: ~30-60 MB per instance
Impact: Significant
Critical factor - shared dependencies:

The real multiplier is common libraries:

Spring Framework
Apache Commons
Logging frameworks (Log4j, SLF4J)
JSON/XML parsers
Guava, etc.
If 5 plugins all include Guava (2.5 MB), you're paying 12.5 MB instead of 2.5 MB.

Mitigation strategies:

Strategy 1: Shared library classloader (hybrid approach):

java
public class SharedLibraryClassLoader extends URLClassLoader {
    private static final Set<String> SHARED_LIBRARIES = Set.of(
        "org.apache.commons",
        "com.google.guava",
        "org.slf4j",
        "com.fasterxml.jackson"
        // etc.
    );
    
    public SharedLibraryClassLoader(URL[] urls, 
                                    ClassLoader parent,
                                    ClassLoader sharedLibs) {
        super(urls, parent);
        this.sharedLibs = sharedLibs;
    }
    
    @Override
    protected Class<?> loadClass(String name, boolean resolve) 
            throws ClassNotFoundException {
        // Check if this is a shared library class
        for (String prefix : SHARED_LIBRARIES) {
            if (name.startsWith(prefix)) {
                return sharedLibs.loadClass(name);
            }
        }
        
        // Otherwise use isolated loading
        return super.loadClass(name, resolve);
    }
}

// Usage:
URLClassLoader sharedLibsLoader = new URLClassLoader(
    new URL[] {
        new File("shared-libs/guava.jar").toURI().toURL(),
        new File("shared-libs/commons-lang3.jar").toURI().toURL()
    }
);

// Each plugin uses shared libs for common dependencies
ClassLoader pluginLoader = new SharedLibraryClassLoader(
    pluginUrls, 
    null, 
    sharedLibsLoader
);
Strategy 2: Lazy loading:

Only load plugins when actually used:

java
public class LazyPlugin {
    private File pluginDir;
    private ClassLoader loader;
    private boolean loaded = false;
    
    public void launch() {
        if (!loaded) {
            loader = createClassLoader(pluginDir);
            loaded = true;
        }
        // Use plugin...
    }
    
    public void unload() {
        if (loaded) {
            loader = null; // Allow GC
            loaded = false;
        }
    }
}
Strategy 3: Preference external processes for heavy apps:

java
public LaunchMode determineBestLaunchMode(PluginDescriptor desc) {
    long totalSize = calculateTotalSize(desc);
    
    if (totalSize > 20_000_000) { // 20 MB
        return LaunchMode.EXTERNAL_PROCESS;
    }
    
    if (hasNativeLibraries(desc)) {
        return LaunchMode.EXTERNAL_PROCESS;
    }
    
    return LaunchMode.ISOLATED_JVM;
}
Strategy 4: Plugin unloading:

java
public void closePlugin(String pluginId) {
    LoadedPlugin plugin = loadedPlugins.remove(pluginId);
    if (plugin != null) {
        plugin.close(); // Close classloader
        System.gc(); // Suggest GC (not guaranteed)
    }
}
Practical recommendations for your desktop:

For lightweight plugins (< 5 MB):

✅ Use isolated classloaders
Memory cost is acceptable
Isolation benefits outweigh overhead
For medium plugins (5-20 MB):

✅ Consider shared library classloader for common deps
Balance isolation vs. efficiency
User can choose via launch mode
For heavy plugins (> 20 MB) or many simultaneous plugins:

✅ Default to external process
Much cleaner memory isolation
OS handles lifecycle
Monitoring approach:

java
public class PluginMemoryMonitor {
    public void reportMemoryUsage() {
        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
        long usedMemory = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        
        System.out.println("Memory usage: " + 
            (usedMemory / 1_000_000) + " MB");
        System.out.println("Loaded plugins: " + 
            loadedPlugins.size());
    }
}
```

**Real-world numbers (approximate):**
```
Desktop app baseline: 50-100 MB
+ 3 small isolated plugins: +300-600 KB (negligible)
+ 2 medium isolated plugins: +10-20 MB (noticeable)
+ 1 large isolated plugin: +30-60 MB (significant)

vs. External processes:
Desktop app: 50-100 MB
+ 3 small external processes: +150-300 MB (3x overhead each)
+ 2 medium external processes: +200-400 MB
+ 1 large external process: +100-200 MB
The tradeoff:

Isolated classloaders: Lower absolute memory, but duplicated classes
External processes: Higher absolute memory (full JVM each), but cleaner isolation
My recommendation for your architecture:

java
public enum LaunchMode {
    EXTERNAL_PROCESS,    // Heavy apps, maximum isolation
    ISOLATED_JVM,        // Light-medium apps, good isolation
    SHARED_LIBS_JVM,     // Medium apps with common deps
    SHARED_JVM           // Trusted lightweight apps only
}
Allow user to choose, but suggest:

Small plugins (< 2 MB): ISOLATED_JVM
Medium (2-10 MB): SHARED_LIBS_JVM or ISOLATED_JVM
Large (> 10 MB): EXTERNAL_PROCESS
Multiple instances of same plugin: SHARED_LIBS_JVM
Bottom line: Yes, there's overhead, but it's manageable for small-to-medium plugins. The hybrid approach (shared libs + isolation) gives you the best of both worlds for your use case.

Does this help you decide on your default strategy?

yes, that is very useful and aligns with my pre-conceptions.  it also reminds me that most plugins would actually be lazy loaded; i.e. since they represent "apps in a desktop", we initially only need to provide a way to launch the plugin (icon, menu, etc.) and would want to defer the actual loading until the user wants to use the plugin/app. (no need to generate code about launching/user interface - I will be covering that later).

Perfect! Yes, exactly - lazy loading is a natural fit for your desktop metaphor. The desktop only needs to:

At startup: Read registry, show icons/menu items (lightweight metadata only)
On user action: Actually load and launch the plugin
This keeps your desktop's initial memory footprint minimal and scales well as users add more plugins.

Key insight: With lazy loading, even the "memory overhead" discussion becomes less critical since you're only paying the cost for plugins the user actually launches, not all registered plugins.

This also means you have more flexibility with the isolated classloader approach since you're not loading dozens of plugins simultaneously at startup.

Sounds like you have a solid architecture plan. Let me know when you want to dive into the launching/UI aspects or any other part of the system!


